      "seed": 982637763,
      "version": 1361,
      "versionNonce": 1523813933,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1785742859212,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "There are multiple child classes of ApplicationContext\neach help in developing diffrent types of application\nsuch as Deskstop and Web application etc .\n\nWorking of Spring Container\n=========================\n\nContainer need two things from user to create bean \n1. pojo class or java bean class .\n2. Configuration --> some information about classes \n(supporting document) which tells the spring \n\nWhich classes should be managed as beans.\nHow to create those objects.\nWhat dependency to inject.\nWhat property values to assign.\nWhat bean scope and lifecycle to use.\n\nthree ways of configuration are:\n1. xml\n2. java class\n3. Annotations\n\nConfiguration is like a script or instruction file that tells \nthe Spring IoC Container what to do. \n\nSpring beans live in a special area of heap called \nas spring context.\n\n\nwhat happens:\n\n1. spring reads configuration and create beans object .\n\n2.  It manage lifecycle of beans .\n\n3. When application/programmer needs a bean it gets the managed \nobject from container .\n\n ** Spring container === Inversion of control \n\nIt manage objects and there dependencies so that \nwe can focus on business logic .\n\n\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "There are multiple child classes of ApplicationContext\neach help in developing diffrent types of application\nsuch as Deskstop and Web application etc .\n\nWorking of Spring Container\n=========================\n\nContainer need two things from user to create bean \n1. pojo class or java bean class .\n2. Configuration --> some information about classes \n(supporting document) which tells the spring \n\nWhich classes should be managed as beans.\nHow to create those objects.\nWhat dependency to inject.\nWhat property values to assign.\nWhat bean scope and lifecycle to use.\n\nthree ways of configuration are:\n1. xml\n2. java class\n3. Annotations\n\nConfiguration is like a script or instruction file that tells \nthe Spring IoC Container what to do. \n\nSpring beans live in a special area of heap called \nas spring context.\n\n\nwhat happens:\n\n1. spring reads configuration and create beans object .\n\n2.  It manage lifecycle of beans .\n\n3. When application/programmer needs a bean it gets the managed \nobject from container .\n\n ** Spring container === Inversion of control \n\nIt manage objects and there dependencies so that \nwe can focus on business logic .\n\n\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
